        <div class="of-ftype__slide" data-slide="5">
          <img src="/images/re-slider-lowe.jpg" alt="Low-E climate control film on historic Philadelphia retail building" class="of-ftype__bg" />
          <div class="of-ftype__overlay"></div>
          <div class="of-ftype__content">
            <div class="of-ftype__step">06</div>
            <h2 class="of-ftype__title">Low-E Climate Control Film</h2>
            <p class="of-ftype__desc">Provides year-round insulation — reflecting solar heat in summer and retaining interior warmth in winter. Particularly effective for Philadelphia retailers in historic buildings with single-pane glass.</p>
            <div class="of-ftype__faq">
              <div class="of-ftype__faq-item">
                <button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">How is Low-E film different from standard solar control film?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button>
                <div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>Standard solar control film blocks incoming solar heat in summer. Low-E film does that and also reflects interior heat back into your store in winter — acting like a second thermal barrier on single-pane glass. For Philadelphia retailers in historic buildings that run high heating bills from October through March, Low-E delivers year-round energy savings that solar control film alone doesn't provide.</div>
              </div>
              <div class="of-ftype__faq-item">
                <button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">Will it change the look of my historic storefront windows?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button>
                <div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>3M Thinsulate CC75 — the most commonly specified Low-E film for historic commercial buildings — has a visible light transmission of 75% and very low exterior reflectance. It reads as essentially clear glass from the street. Philadelphia's preservation districts (Old City, Society Hill) accept Low-E film as a non-invasive window treatment that doesn't alter the historic character of the facade.</div>
              </div>
              <div class="of-ftype__faq-item">
                <button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">Does Low-E film work on single-pane glass?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button>
                <div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>Yes — Low-E film was specifically developed to improve the thermal performance of existing single-pane glass without replacing it. Many Philadelphia storefronts in pre-war buildings still have original single-pane glass that transfers heat aggressively. Low-E film is the most effective retrofit available for that situation short of full window replacement, which is often impractical or prohibited in historic districts.</div>
              </div>
            </div>
            <a href="/contact/" class="of-ftype__cta">Get a Low-E film estimate →</a>
